// `@doc("...")`: what a declaration is for, in prose.
//
// `///` lines are sugar for it: the parser turns the run of them above a
// declaration — an item, a field, a variant, a trait member — into one `@doc`
// whose `text` is the lines without their slashes, joined by newlines. Written
// out, it is an ordinary attribute:
//
//     @doc("The number of elements.")
//     len :: func (self: *Self) -> usize { ... }
//
// The compiler finds it by its `#lang` tag, so `///` means this attribute in
// every file whether or not the name `doc` is in scope there, and a program's
// own `doc` does not change what a doc comment is. The language server shows
// the text on hover, `nestc --emit metadata` writes it out, and reflection reads
// it like any attribute (`attr_of.<doc>(m.attrs)`).
@public(all) @attribute doc :: #lang("doc") struct { text: str }
